Journal Entry – Week 2
Defining Intelligence
Part 1 — Initial Speculative Definition of Intelligence
1.1 Initial Provocations from Class
“Is intelligence the ability to solve problems or the ability to adapt?”

“Can a machine be intelligent if it doesn’t understand what it’s doing?”

“Is intelligence about reasoning, learning, or something else entirely?”

1.2 Initial Speculative Definition
Before engaging with the readings, I would define intelligence as:

“The capacity of a system to learn from experience, adapt to new situations, and use information to achieve goals.”

This definition feels intuitive because it applies to both humans and machines. It emphasizes learning, adaptation, and goal‑directed behavior without assuming biological or conscious processes.

Part 2 — Core Readings Reflection
2.1 Legg & Hutter — A Collection of Definitions of Intelligence
Legg & Hutter compile over 70 definitions from psychology, AI, philosophy, and biology. Several themes recur:

Intelligence involves achieving goals.

It requires adaptation across environments.

It often includes learning, reasoning, or problem‑solving.

Some definitions emphasize prediction or optimization.

Their unified proposal — intelligence as an agent’s ability to achieve goals in a wide range of environments — highlights generality and flexibility. This broadened my view beyond learning alone.

2.2 Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (SEP)
The SEP entry on Artificial Intelligence emphasizes:

Competence in reasoning, planning, learning, and perception

Differences between symbolic and sub‑symbolic approaches

Intelligence as behavioral performance, not internal consciousness

This challenged my initial definition by showing that intelligence also involves structured representation and inference, not just adaptation.

2.3 Alan Turing — Computing Machinery and Intelligence
Turing reframes intelligence as an operational property: if a machine’s behavior is indistinguishable from a human’s in conversation, it can be considered intelligent.

Key insights:

Intelligence can be evaluated through performance, not subjective experience.

The Imitation Game uses human‑like behavior as a benchmark.

Some intelligent systems might fail the Turing Test despite being capable.

Turing argues that intelligence should not be restricted to biological systems.

This pushed me to think about intelligence as something observable and testable.

2.4 Additional Research
I added the following source to the shared Zotero library:

Russell & Norvig (2021). Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach.

This text frames intelligence as rational action — selecting actions that maximize expected performance given available information. This helped me refine my rule‑based model by emphasizing decision‑making and rationality.

Part 3 — Constructing a Theory of Intelligence
3.1 Rule‑Based Analytical Definition
Rule 1 — Goal‑Directed Behavior
The system must pursue one or more goals, either explicitly programmed or internally generated.

Rule 2 — Information Processing
The system must acquire, store, and use information to guide its actions.

Rule 3 — Learning or Adaptation
The system must improve its performance or modify its behavior based on experience or environmental feedback.

Rule 4 — Generality Across Contexts
The system must be able to operate in more than one environment or task domain.

Rule 5 — Flexible Problem‑Solving
The system must be capable of selecting among multiple possible actions, not merely executing a fixed script.

Boundary Between Non‑Intelligent and Intelligent
A system becomes intelligent when it satisfies Rules 1–3.
It becomes more intelligent as it satisfies Rules 4–5 to a greater degree.

Distinguishing Intelligence from Agency
Intelligence: the ability to learn, adapt, and solve problems

Agency: the ability to initiate actions based on goals or preferences

A system may be intelligent without agency (e.g., a learning algorithm with no autonomy), or agentic without intelligence (e.g., a thermostat).

3.2 Boundary Testing
Systems Classified as Intelligent
1. Humans
Goal‑directed

Learn from experience

Adapt across environments

Flexible problem‑solving

High generality
Classification: Intelligent

2. Large Language Models
Process information

Learn from data during training

Adapt responses to context

Generalize across tasks

Limited agency
Classification: Intelligent but not agentic

Systems Classified as Not Intelligent
1. Mechanical Clock
No learning

No adaptation

No flexible problem‑solving

Executes fixed rules
Classification: Not intelligent

2. Basic If‑Then Rule System
Has a goal (e.g., filter spam)

No learning

No adaptation

No generality
Classification: Not intelligent

Ambiguities and Limitations
Some systems (e.g., thermostats, bacteria) show minimal adaptation but not enough to meet the threshold.

Machine learning systems “learn” differently from humans, raising questions about whether learning requires understanding.

Intelligence may be scalar, and these rules do not quantify degrees of intelligence.

Brief Ethical Reflection
Defining intelligence has ethical implications. Systems classified as intelligent may be granted autonomy, trust, or responsibility. Over‑attributing intelligence to machines can lead to misplaced expectations or unsafe delegation of decision‑making. Under‑attributing intelligence can limit useful applications or misrepresent capabilities.
